            {
               "text": "<p class=\"unitaryCircle\" id=\"circtri0\"><style>.circle-draw circle, .circle-draw line {stroke: #71a6d2; stroke-width: 4; stroke-linecap: round; fill: none;} .circle-draw circle { stroke-dasharray: 942; stroke-dashoffset: 942; animation: drawCircle 2s linear forwards;} .radius-line { transform-origin: 300px 170px; animation: rotateRadius 2s linear forwards;} .radius-45 { opacity: 0; animation: showRadius45 0.3s ease-out forwards; animation-delay: 2s; } .radius-30{ opacity: 0; animation: showRadius30 0.3s ease-out forwards; animation-delay: 2s; } .vertical45 { opacity: 0; animation: showVertical45 0.3s ease-out forwards; animation-delay: 2.5s; } .atlasTextRadius { opacity: 0; animation: atlasTextRadiusMov 0.3s ease-out forwards; animation-delay: 2.8s; }</style><style>@keyframes drawCircle { to {stroke-dashoffset: 0; } } @keyframes rotateRadius { from { transform: rotate(0deg); } to   { transform: rotate(-360deg); } } @keyframes showRadius45 { from { opacity: 0; } to { opacity: 1; } } @keyframes showRadius30 { from { opacity: 0; } to { opacity: 1; } }  @keyframes showVertical45 { from { opacity: 0; } to { opacity: 1; } }  @keyframes atlasTextRadiusMov { from { opacity: 0; } to { opacity: 1; } }</style><div class=\"atlasCircleContainer\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 600 340\" class=\"circle-draw\" id=\"svgcirctri0\">  <defs> <marker id=\"arrow\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"5\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"3\" markerHeight=\"3\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"> <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\" /></marker> </defs> <path class=\"radius-30\" d=\"M 320 147 A 30 30 0 0 1 340.0 170.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#71a6d2\" stroke-width=\"3\"/> <line x1=\"300\" y1=\"169\" x2=\"470\" y2=\"169\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" style=\"stroke: black;\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\" /> <line x1=\"300\" y1=\"169\" x2=\"300\" y2=\"8\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" style=\"stroke: black;\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\" /> <line x1=\"300\" y1=\"169\" x2=\"130\" y2=\"169\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" style=\"stroke: black;\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\" /> <line x1=\"300\" y1=\"169\" x2=\"300\" y2=\"330\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" style=\"stroke: black;\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\" /> <circle cx=\"300\" cy=\"170\" r=\"148\" transform=\"scale(-1 1) translate(-600 0)  rotate(180 300 170)\"></circle> <line class=\"radius-line\" x1=\"300\" y1=\"169\" x2=\"448\" y2=\"169\"></line> <line class=\"radius-45\" x1=\"300\" y1=\"169\" x2=\"406\" y2=\"66\"></line>  <line class=\"vertical45\" x1=\"406\" y1=\"66\" x2=\"406\" y2=\"166\"></line> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"360\" y=\"188\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">c</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"360\" y=\"88\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">c</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"360\" y=\"158\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">a</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"390\" y=\"128\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">b</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"320\" y=\"165\" font-size=\"1.0em\" font-weight=\"bold\">θ</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"282\" y=\"187\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">O</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"407\" y=\"65\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">P</text>  <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"405\" y=\"182\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">Q</text></svg></div></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Graph 1</b>: Circle with a radius of 1 containing a right triangle.</p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Trigonometry (Cosine)\n\nIn the previous text, we explored how to draw a right triangle and saw that, from the center **O**, we could reach any point **P** on the circle by traveling along the legs of the triangle or along the hypotenuse.\n\nWe also learned that, as a ray moves away from the horizontal line, an angle theta (θ) opens up between them — an angle we have not explored yet. In this text, we will connect that angle to the legs of the triangle. We begin with a reflection:",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 1: Connecting the angle with the legs</b><br /><br />We know that the angle theta (θ) represents the opening of the ray, and that this ray is the hypotenuse of the right triangle.<br /><br />How can we connect this angle to the legs?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Trigonometry\n\nBefore establishing the relationship between the angle, the legs, and the hypotenuse, let's observe exactly what happens as the ray (the hypotenuse of the right triangle) rotates to draw the circle. Our relationships must reproduce precisely what we observe.\n\nLet's list everything the relationships need to capture:\n1. The horizontal leg **a** reaches its greatest length when the ray lies along the horizontal. The vertical leg **b** reaches its greatest length when the ray lies along the vertical.\n2. The angle theta (θ) grows from the horizontal, counterclockwise (from right to left).\n3. Each leg reaches its smallest length precisely when the other leg is at its maximum: leg **a** is shortest when leg **b** is longest, and vice versa.\n4. In all of these situations, the ray (the hypotenuse) keeps a single, constant length. The legs reach their greatest values exactly when they equal the length of the ray.\n\nThe detailed study of these relationships is known as trigonometry (<htcite0>). It is carried out through trigonometric functions, the first of which we introduce next.\n\n#### Cosine\n\nThe first trigonometric function we introduce is the cosine, defined in **Formula 1** (<htcite1>). There, the *adjacent leg* is the leg that, together with the hypotenuse, forms the angle theta (θ) — leg **a** in our figures:",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "0098b5fe-1cb7-4451-82c9-8e5d5d4f20e6",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "Trigonometry",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "92e66bd4-1d7f-4f28-bed2-f62210493341",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "Cossine",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>cos (θ)</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mfrac><mi>adjacent leg</mi><mi>hypotenuse</mi></mfrac></math></p></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Formula 1</b>: Definition of cosine.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 2: The definition of cosine</b><br /><br />Does the definition of the cosine presented in <b>Formula 1</b> manage to capture everything we listed about leg <b>a</b>, the angle, and the hypotenuse?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### The Maximum Value\n\nTo answer this reflection, we need to check every item in the list above. To keep things simple, we will isolate from **Graph 1** the parts that match each item.\n\nStart with the ray **c** lying exactly on the horizontal line: leg **a** then has the same length as the ray (**Graph 2**):",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<div class=\"atlasCircleContainer\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 600 170\" class=\"circle-draw\" id=\"svgcirctri0\">  <defs> <marker id=\"arrow\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"5\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"3\" markerHeight=\"3\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"> <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\" /></marker> </defs> <path d=\"M 348 150 A 148 148 0 0 0 200 2\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#71a6d2\" stroke-width=\"3\"/> <line x1=\"200\" y1=\"150\" x2=\"370\" y2=\"150\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" style=\"stroke: black;\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\" /> <line class=\"radius-line\" x1=\"200\" y1=\"149\" x2=\"348\" y2=\"149\"></line> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"260\" y=\"147\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">c</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"180\" y=\"149\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">O</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"350\" y=\"149\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">P</text>  <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"260\" y=\"165\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">a</text> </svg></div></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Graph 2</b>: The hypotenuse lying on the adjacent leg: the angle theta (θ) is zero.</p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Since leg **a** has the same length as the ray **c** in **Graph 2**, that is, **a = c**, and the angle theta (θ) is zero (the ray does not separate from the horizontal at all), the cosine reaches its greatest possible value: 1, as shown in **Calculation 1** (<htcite0>):",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "112e532d-e8af-40d6-b1b5-527978ee42f3",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Two sides",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>cos (θ)</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mfrac><mi>adjacent leg</mi><mi>hypotenuse</mi></mfrac><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mfrac><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi></mfrac><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mfrac><mi>c</mi><mi>c</mi></mfrac><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Calculation 1</b>: Maximum value obtained for the cosine of theta (θ).</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Intermediate Values\n\nWhen the radius begins to rotate, the angle theta (θ) starts to assume a value different from zero, as shown in **Graph 3**.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<div class=\"atlasCircleContainer\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 600 160\" class=\"circle-draw\" id=\"svgcirctri0\">  <defs> <marker id=\"arrow\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"5\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"3\" markerHeight=\"3\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"> <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\" /></marker> </defs> <path d=\"M 348 150 A 148 148 0 0 0 200 2\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#71a6d2\" stroke-width=\"3\"/> <path class=\"radius-30\" d=\"M 230 130 A 30 30 0 0 1 240.0 150.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#71a6d2\" stroke-width=\"3\"/> <line x1=\"200\" y1=\"150\" x2=\"370\" y2=\"150\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" style=\"stroke: black;\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\" /> <line class=\"radius-line\" x1=\"200\" y1=\"149\" x2=\"320\" y2=\"149\"></line> <line class=\"radius-line\" x1=\"319\" y1=\"149\" x2=\"320\" y2=\"67\"></line> <line class=\"radius-line\" x1=\"200\" y1=\"149\" x2=\"319\" y2=\"67\"></line> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"250\" y=\"89\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">c</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"180\" y=\"150\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">O</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"325\" y=\"65\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">P</text>  <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"260\" y=\"146\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">a</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"223\" y=\"146\" font-size=\"1.0em\" font-weight=\"bold\">θ</text> </svg></div></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Graph 3</b>: The angle theta (θ) opening between the hypotenuse and the adjacent leg.</p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Another consequence of this rotation is that leg **a** gets shorter. This is no coincidence: leg **a** is the projection of the hypotenuse **c** onto the horizontal axis — its \"shadow\" on that line.\n\nBoth behaviors we have just described affect the cosine of theta (θ) directly. As leg **a** (the projection) shrinks, the cosine shrinks with it, because we are dividing a smaller leg by the same constant: the hypotenuse.\n\n#### The Minimum Value\n\nThe smallest value we can see in the graphs on this page occurs when leg **a** has length zero. That happens whenever the radius (our hypotenuse) is perfectly vertical, as shown in **Graph 4**:",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<div class=\"atlasCircleContainer\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 600 160\" class=\"circle-draw\" id=\"svgcirctri0\">  <defs> <marker id=\"arrow\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"5\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"3\" markerHeight=\"3\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"> <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\" /></marker> </defs> <path d=\"M 348 150 A 148 148 0 0 0 200 2\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#71a6d2\" stroke-width=\"3\"/> <path class=\"radius-30\" d=\"M 200 120 A 30 30 0 0 1 230.0 150.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#71a6d2\" stroke-width=\"3\"/> <line x1=\"200\" y1=\"150\" x2=\"370\" y2=\"150\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" style=\"stroke: black;\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\" /> <line class=\"radius-line\" x1=\"200\" y1=\"149\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"4\"></line> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"180\" y=\"89\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">c</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"180\" y=\"150\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">O</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"180\" y=\"15\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">P</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"208\" y=\"146\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">θ</text> </svg></div></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Graph 4</b>: The radius standing completely vertical, so the adjacent leg has length zero.</p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "One more point about **Graph 4**: even though the projection of the radius onto the horizontal axis is zero when the radius is vertical, the angle theta (θ) is still there — it has simply reached 90°.\n\n#### Conclusions\n\nThe cosine of theta (θ) captures exactly the projection of leg **a** as the radius rotates around the center **O**. Formula 1 already contains this fact; multiplying both sides by the hypotenuse (the radius **c**) makes it explicit:",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>cos (θ)</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mfrac><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi></mfrac></math></p> <p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>(c) cos (θ)</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mfrac><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi></mfrac><mn>(c)</mn></math></p> <p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>(c) cos (θ)</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>a (1)</mn></math></p> <p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>(c) cos (θ)</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>a</mn></math></p> <p class=\"desc\"><b>Calculation 2</b>: Projection of the hypotenuse onto the horizontal axis.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "From **Calculation 2**, we can confirm the items in the list from the *Trigonometry* section above:\n- **Line 1**: Here leg **a** grows outward from the center **O** until it reaches the greatest length possible inside the circle: the radius **c**. At that point the cosine equals 1. In the other direction, as leg **a** shrinks from the edge of the circle, the cosine falls until it reaches zero, where the projection has shrunk to the center **O**. This description matches items *1, 3 and 4* of the *Trigonometry* section.\n- **Line 2**: The radius **c** acts as a scale factor on both sides of the calculation. To see how the leg depends on the radius and on the angle, we add that factor to both sides — that is, we multiply both sides by **c**.\n- **Line 3**: On the right side we were already dividing by the radius, so multiplying by it again leaves **c/c**, which equals 1. That is why the right side becomes **a (1)**.\n- **Line 4**: Finally, this line says that as the cosine gets smaller, leg **a** gets shorter: the angle is growing, so the projection **a** shrinks. This last line corresponds exactly to *item 2* of the list in the *Trigonometry* section.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Practicing\n\nNow it is time to practice what we have just learned. In **Graph 2**, the radius was on the horizontal line: there, leg **a** had the same length as the radius **c**, and the cosine of theta (θ) reached its greatest value: 1. From that position, as the radius rotates counterclockwise, leg **a** shrinks and the cosine decreases, until it reaches zero when the radius is completely vertical.\n\nClick on the chart to move the radius inside the first quadrant. Watch the right triangle, the angle theta (θ), and the value of the cosine change. Move the radius to different positions and try to anticipate the value of the cosine before reading it in the panel below the chart. For example, when the angle is 60°, the cosine is exactly 0.5 — see if you can find that position.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"cscPracticeInstruction\" style=\"text-align:center;\">Click on the chart to move the radius in the first quadrant. Observe how the adjacent leg (and the cosine) shrink as the angle theta (θ) grows.</p> <div class=\"atlasCircleContainer\"><div id=\"cospractice8fcc\"></div></div> <p class=\"desc\"><b>Graph 5</b>: Interactive practice: moving the radius in the first quadrant, the cosine decreases as the angle theta (θ) grows.</p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      }
